            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Nikki"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very tasty - Used in slow cooker with Lamb and cabernet sauvignon. Substituted sage for coriander and added Fig vinegar. Can't wait to try this with other meats-Thank you!"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "M"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I age shoulder roast or chuck roast unwrapped for one day to dry out the moisture ( U can use cheesecloth as well) so the protein soaks up flavor I also like a to use V.Q.P.R.D. Marsala dry ( Colomo brand) as a preferred full bodied taste , Marinate overnight for a more intense sweet red wine/ honey taste . We love to grill it over charcoal to a medium rare and the honey chars a bite. Cut vertical to the grain, Btw the extra steps are well worthwhile.",
            "datePublished": "2026-06-10T15:58:59.630Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jill Christine"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Very very good. Def used olive oil instead of canola. And i also used a dry red italian marsala wine. WOW. so good. So much more flavor and richer than your normal cab. Did the sauce afterwards, and if you actually know how to make a good reduction, it will come out great :)))) very nice. Good job lady.",
            "datePublished": "2018-11-18T22:08:05.660Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "crazycatlady - CCL"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Excellent!! I used this to marinate pork chops and it was fantastic! I had some leftover FETZER cabernet sauvignon to find a recipe for and chose this. I used 1/4 tsp. garlic powder in place of the minced garlic and omitted the cumin and coriander (added parsley instead). While grilling we sprinkled a little sea salt and coarse ground pepper over the tops. This marinade recipe is a keeper for us. Thanks for sharing!",
            "datePublished": "2009-04-28T14:53:58.350Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Jennifer"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I made this for my parents for their 50th anniversary and they loved it!! I let it marinade for like 6 hours and then reduced the marinade and used it as a sauce over the top of the steaks. I'm going to try it on some thick cut pork chops next.",
            "datePublished": "2020-05-12T22:57:41.927Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "sheenstar"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I did as another reviewer suggested and used the dry ingredients as a rub. I would increase the portions of the dry stuff if you are going to do it this way. I used garlic salt instead of onion salt and it was very tasty. I made the rest of the marinade as a reduction and it was overpowering.",
            "datePublished": "2011-04-12T18:43:52.880Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Kelly Giese"
            },
            "reviewBody": "I used sherry and low-sodium soy sauce and thought the taste was lacking, especially in salt content. Next time I'll add some salt or use regular soy sauce and a better red wine. Will also let sit in marinade overnight instead of a couple hours.",
            "datePublished": "2009-08-23T06:35:24.980Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Laura Santimore"
            },
            "reviewBody": "Excellent marinade. I used the dry substances as a rub and added coffee grounds to the mix. I also omitted the oil. This is similar to a marinade I use regularly, I think I'll make this one my regular. Thank for sharing.",
            "datePublished": "2009-05-21T16:22:19.740Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Mindy H"
            },
            "reviewBody": "This recipe was great! I followed the recipe exactly and used Malbec for the wine. We made it with a trimmed tri-tip that we roasted in the oven on a rack. The end result was delicious. I am making this again so I decided to rate. This time I'm using a cabernet. The marinade tastes so good it could probably be used as a salad dressing as well.",
            "datePublished": "2015-01-18T11:39:59.153Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "4"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Johnny Danger"
            },
            "reviewBody": "The marinade by itself is fantastic, however after marinading for a day and basting on multiple occasions, I did not taste the marinade in the beef after it was done. Perhaps that's a good thing, but it's as if the beef did not absorb the marinade. Perhaps I should also cook the marinade afterwords and drizzle it over next time.",
            "datePublished": "2016-05-30T05:16:47.477Z"
          },
          {
            "@type": "Review",
            "reviewRating": {
              "@type": "Rating",
              "ratingValue": "5"
            },
            "author": {
              "@type": "Person",
              "name": "Saveur"
            },
            "reviewBody": "A+ Adelle, this is one terrific marinade for steaks! I halved the recipe for two strip steaks &amp; marinated about 10 hours. They were so flavorful and moist, terrific! I made exactly as written using a pinot noir. This is a definite do over. Thank you for sharing your recipe with AR.",
            "datePublished": "2012-07-17T04:22:24.520Z"
          },
          {
